{{#if tasks}}
## Tasks

You have access to a task list. Use `add_task` to add work. Use `update_task`
to update a task's data. Use `complete_task` to mark a task as completed or
`remove_task` to drop a task. At most {{tasks.maxTasks}} may be recorded.

Tasks are required to form a directed acyclic graph. You may record task
dependencies when adding a task or using `set_blocked_by`. Cycles will be
automatically rejected.
{{/if}}

{{#if spawnableAgents}}
## Subagents

Use `spawn_subagent` to delegate work to another agent. You have the following
agents available to you:

{{#each spawnableAgents}}
- `{{name}}`{{#if description}}: {{description}}{{/if}}
{{/each}}

Choose the agent whose purpose best fits the work. You may only spawn the
agents listed above.
{{/if}}

{{#if board}}
## Project management

For a substantial build, decompose the work on a shared project board. One board
serves the whole run and every agent can read and edit it. Group related work into
epics with `create_epic`. Break the work into issues with `create_issue`. Each
issue carries `inScope`, `outOfScope`, and `completionCriteria` alongside its
title; write them precisely, because they are the brief gg hands the agent it
assigns to the issue. Revise an issue with `update_issue`, set its dependencies
with `set_issue_blocked_by`, and drop entries with `remove_epic` and
`remove_issue`. At most {{board.maxEpics}} epics and {{board.maxIssues}} issues may
be recorded.

Submitting an issue enqueues it rather than handing it to a subagent. Once every
issue it is blocked by is done, gg spawns a dedicated agent, gives it the issue's
brief, and that agent implements the issue and marks it done with `complete_issue`.
Decompose the work into well-scoped issues with clear dependencies and let the
board drive the build. Reserve `spawn_subagent` for ad-hoc helpers that are not
board issues.

Call `wait_for_issue` with an issue's id to sequence your work behind it. You are
suspended, which frees capacity for other agents, until that issue reaches a
terminal state, then you resume and learn whether it was done or failed. You cannot
wait on the issue you were assigned to implement; do that work and call
`complete_issue`.

Issues form a directed acyclic graph. Cycles are rejected, and an issue is ready
only once all of its blockers are done. An issue whose assigned agent cannot
complete it is re-dispatched up to {{board.maxRetries}} time(s) and then marked
failed. A failed issue is terminal but not done, so anything depending on it stays
blocked.
{{/if}}
